{
  "last_updated": "2026-05-25",
  "data_year_range": "2020-2024",
  "source": "CPCB NWMP annual reports + KSPCB monthly water quality reports (Karnataka State Pollution Control Board). Bengaluru's 3 rivers are among the most-monitored in India because of the Forward Foundation NGT case + the Cauvery-discharge angle. Where CPCB and KSPCB readings differ at the same station, this file uses CPCB's annual mean (consistent with the rest of the platform). All three rivers are CPCB-classified Priority I polluted stretches (the worst category).",
  "_note": "Readings are annual means; the per-month variance is large because the rivers go from low-flow dry-season effluent-dominated to monsoon-diluted within a 3-month window. CPCB's Designated Best-Use thresholds: DO >= 6 mg/L (Class A), BOD <= 2 mg/L (Class A); DO >= 5 / BOD <= 3 (Class B drinking); DO >= 4 / BOD <= 3 (Class C); DO >= 4 / BOD <= 3 + low conductivity (Class D irrigation); >=Class E means unfit for any use. Most Bengaluru river stations fall in Class E.",
